WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.650
YUFENG GUO: Are you
looking to run machine

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.140
learning on Docker containers
in the cloud, future proofing

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.240
your workflow and
allowing you to use

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.620
any library of your choice?

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.650
If so, then you've come
to the right place.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.260
Because today, we're going
to run custom containers

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.970
on Google Cloud AI Platform.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.414
[TONES]

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.100
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.650
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.600
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.050
And on this episode, we're going
to use AI Platform's custom

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.130
containers to see how to run
custom training jobs on Google

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.810
Cloud using Docker containers.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.980
This approach gives us a
number of key benefits.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.650
First is faster start up time.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.350
If you use a custom container
with all of your dependencies

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.570
preinstalled, you
can save the time

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.520
that your training
application would otherwise

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.680
take to install those
dependencies when starting up.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.460
So that gives you faster
training and fewer network

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.200
and compute resources used.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.550
Second, you can use any machine
learning framework you want.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.380
Just install your
chosen framework

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.360
in your custom
container and then just

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.910
use it to run those
jobs on AI Platform.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.670
This not only means you can
use every tool under the sun,

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.060
but it also future
proofs your workflow,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.820
allowing you to easily
switch libraries or add

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.880
new ones for experimentation.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.190
Third-- and this is a little bit
related to the previous point--

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.820
you can use the latest
build of a library.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.703
Or if you want an
older version--

00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:33.120
if that's what
you're relying on--

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.140
that's great too.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.490
No one will force you to
update if you're not ready.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:37.953
It's your container.

00:01:37.953 --> 00:01:39.870
It's also worth pointing
out that since you're

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.790
building your own
container image,

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.310
that if you want a
certain functionality,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.000
you're going to
have to install it.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.080
So if you want something like
hyperparameter tuning or GPU

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.660
support, you'll need
to make sure to include

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.810
the necessary libraries to
make those features work.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:55.342
OK.

00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:57.300
Let's look at how you
can use custom containers

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.620
to train a model.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.700
We're going to try to use
PyTorch to train on MNIST,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.200
just to show off the
tooling and process.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.607
First things first, we've
got to create the container.

00:02:06.607 --> 00:02:08.940
Now I know that some parts
of the data science community

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.090
are a bit against learning yet
another tool to do their work.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.630
But I'm hoping that you're
here to learn new things.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.780
Plus, it definitely seems like
containers are here to stay.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.930
So it will be really
useful to learn this tool.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.210
Our Dockerfile installs
PyTorch, Cloud ML Hypertune,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.640
and downloads gsutil for
exporting cloud storage.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.580
And then it
configures some PATs.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.070
Finally, it copies
in the training code

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.750
and then sets it up
to run right when

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.360
the container starts executing.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.310
Be sure to test your
Dockerfile locally

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.270
before you use it by building
that image and running it.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.810
Then, you can push it off to
Google Container Registry,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.100
or GCR.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.710
That's where AI Platform
will look for your image

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.190
during the training job.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.390
Once you have your image
uploaded to GCR, creating a job

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.078
is quite similar to normal
usage of AI Platform Training.

00:03:01.078 --> 00:03:03.120
There's just one extra
command line argument when

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.620
you submit that training job.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.920
You want to make sure you
include --master-image-uri

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.310
and pass in that GCR URI.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.465
At this point you
might be wondering,

00:03:13.465 --> 00:03:15.590
why go through all the
trouble of building a Docker

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.450
image if we could just
run the training locally?

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.640
There is a couple of
scenarios, I can imagine,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.298
where this would be
particularly useful.

00:03:23.298 --> 00:03:25.590
Perhaps your data is larger
than your machine's storage

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.503
or the data set is
secured behind permissions

00:03:27.503 --> 00:03:28.920
so you can't run
the full training

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.110
job on a local machine.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.260
Or maybe the compute resources
of your local machine

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.850
just aren't adequate
because you need

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.460
something like multiple GPUs.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.050
Third reason might
be that you want

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.960
to do distributed training
across many machines,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.380
or you want to run many jobs
in parallel to each other,

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.290
or you just really like
working with containers.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.240
Hey, that's always
a possibility.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.670
For those who need containers,
AI Platform Training

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.790
with custom containers provides
a great new set of features

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.100
and functionality to explore.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.380
I can't wait to hear about how
you containerize your model.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.460
Thanks for watching this episode
of Cloud "AI Adventures."

